/// The typed operation seam the host router invokes once per graph node.
///
/// STATUS: LIVE 0.1.0 (wamn-0h0g.16.2; versioned by wamn-0h0g.16.10). This is
/// the repository's sole definition of `wamn:node@0.1.0`, against the execution
/// model of `docs/exe-model.md` rev 4. This revision drops the
/// payload store, the run/flow/attempt identity, and the durable run context,
/// and replaces `run-context` with `node-context`.
///
/// Wholesale supersession is why the archived package's own compatibility clause
/// ("0.1.x admits only additive or clarifying changes; any breaking change waits
/// for 0.2") is not violated here. That clause governs the EVOLUTION of a live
/// package, and the archived one is not live: it was deleted, and the
/// vendored-copy registry walk in `tests/conformance/src/invocation.rs` proves
/// nothing in the tree binds the archived shape. There is no deployed 0.1.0
/// consumer for a version bump to protect. So the MVP
/// version-identity rule governs instead — every first-party `wamn:*` WIT
/// package sits at @0.1.0 for the MVP identity window
/// (`tests/conformance/src/version_identity.rs`) — and no exception is carved
/// for this package. A reader diffing the archive against this file finds the
/// supersession recorded here, not a silent redefinition.
///
/// SETTLED LOCATION. This package lives with the router crate that invokes it
/// and owns it. Consumers vendor it the usual way, at
/// `<consumer>/wit/deps/wamn-node/package.wit`, and every such copy is
/// registered in the repo-level drift guard before it is bound.
///
/// What the router does with a node is the entire justification for this
/// surface, and nothing here serves any other reader: resolve the wiring's
/// active version, walk the graph in frontier order, acquire a pooled instance
/// keyed by the node's component digest, call `run`, route the emission by port,
/// and classify a `node-error` into retry, throttle, error edge, dead-letter, or
/// non-failure cancellation.
/// Effective-release admission maps each package-scoped logical operation in
/// the exact release composition to one digest plus one exact exported handler
/// instance. The router selects that operation-named instance and invokes its
/// unchanged `run` function; several operations may therefore share bytes.
interface types {
  /// JSON-encoded string. A string, not a structured value, so every guest
  /// language reaches it through its own stock codec; the router never inspects
  /// it. A batched stream delivery (`docs/exe-model.md`, ingress path 2) arrives
  /// as one JSON array in a single invocation, so batching needs no arm here.
  type json = string;

  /// Everything the router knows that a node invocation may need.
  ///
  /// Deliberately carries no secrets and no run history. Credential generations
  /// are host-injected at the effect plugins, so an author never sees a
  /// connection string, and there is no durable per-node fact to thread through.
  ///
  /// The invocation identities are the idempotency basis. Delivery is
  /// at-least-once (`docs/exe-model.md` R2), so a component's own effect
  /// idempotency is authored rather than platform-provided, and the author
  /// derives a dedup key from this record — the same key `emit` presents at the
  /// boundary for producer dedup.
  record node-context {
    /// The wiring this delivery is running. With `wiring-version` it scopes an
    /// authored dedup key to the graph revision that asked for the effect, so a
    /// pointer flip to a corrected wiring is not suppressed by the superseded
    /// one's key.
    wiring-id: string,
    /// The active wiring version the router resolved for this delivery. Wirings
    /// are versioned, gated data hot-activated by pointer flip; a delivery runs
    /// against exactly one version, resolved once at its start.
    wiring-version: u32,
    /// This node's instance id within the wiring graph. Two instances of the
    /// same component in one wiring are distinct effects and must not share a
    /// dedup key.
    node-id: string,
    /// The delivery being walked: stable across redeliveries of the same
    /// ingress event (queue lease expiry, JetStream nack), distinct per event.
    /// `(delivery-id, node-id, occurrence)` is therefore the stable, unique
    /// basis for one node visit's external effect under at-least-once
    /// redelivery.
    delivery-id: string,
    /// The target input selected by the incoming wiring edge. The wiring entry
    /// has no incoming edge and therefore no target input.
    input-port: option<string>,
    /// Which visit of this node in this delivery is running. Merge and loop
    /// visits increment it; retries of one visit retain it.
    occurrence: u32,
    /// W3C trace context. OTel is the record in this model — the router opens
    /// one span per component invocation and there are no durable node facts to
    /// fall back on — so a node making outbound calls MUST propagate this when
    /// present, or the trace is the only evidence and it breaks at the node.
    traceparent: option<string>,
    tracestate: option<string>,
    /// Remaining budget for this invocation in milliseconds. The host enforces a
    /// hard deadline regardless (epoch interruption); this lets a well-behaved
    /// node bound its own client timeouts and stop cooperatively — it is what
    /// makes `node-error.cancelled` reachable without a cancellation import.
    deadline-ms: option<u64>,
    /// This node's declared parameters as a JSON document, template-expanded by
    /// the router. The generic standard-library components are configured
    /// entirely through it — the `entity` component receives its relation and
    /// filter spec here rather than through a per-relation generated type.
    config: json,
  }

  /// A failure as the error edge and the invocation span report it.
  record error-detail {
    message: string,
    /// Machine-readable code for error-edge routing and studio display, e.g.
    /// "ECONNREFUSED", "HTTP_429". Distinct from `message`, which is prose.
    code: option<string>,
  }

  record rate-limit-detail {
    detail: error-detail,
    /// Source-authoritative delay, e.g. from a `Retry-After` header. This is the
    /// only thing that distinguishes `rate-limited` from `retryable`: absent, the
    /// router falls back to its own backoff and the two cases coincide.
    retry-after-ms: option<u64>,
  }

  /// How a node invocation failed or stopped without emission, in the terms the
  /// router acts on — never by matching strings. Each case selects a different
  /// router action or the explicit non-failure cancellation disposition.
  variant node-error {
    /// Transient. The router retries within the delivery's budget, or lets the
    /// delivery redeliver (queue lease expiry, JetStream nack).
    retryable(error-detail),
    /// The upstream system signalled throttling. Retryable like the above, but
    /// the router honours the source's own delay instead of its backoff curve.
    rate-limited(rate-limit-detail),
    /// Permanent for this input. The router stops retrying and routes down the
    /// wiring's error edge.
    terminal(error-detail),
    /// The input violated this node's declared contract. Never retried and never
    /// merely error-edged: shape safety is the gate's jurisdiction, so reaching
    /// this at runtime means the payload is poison — it dead-letters (stream
    /// path) rather than being redelivered forever against the same bad input.
    invalid-input(error-detail),
    /// The node stopped cooperatively, typically on its own `deadline-ms`, and
    /// did not complete. The router ends the walk as cancelled without an
    /// error edge, failure, or terminal verdict.
    cancelled,
  }

  /// What a successful invocation produced: the payload and the output port
  /// selecting which edges the router routes it down.
  record emission {
    payload: json,
    /// Absent means the default "main" port. Branch nodes emit named ports; the
    /// reserved "error" port is never emitted here, because errors travel as
    /// `node-error` and the router routes them.
    port: option<string>,
  }
}

/// The structural handler contract. Each admitted operation exports an
/// operation-named interface with this exact `run` shape; the operation token
/// selects that instance. A node is a pure function of (ctx, input, granted
/// host imports), so a pooled instance remains safely reusable.
interface handler {
  use types.{json, node-context, emission, node-error};

  run: func(ctx: node-context, input: json) -> result<emission, node-error>;
}

/// The async variant of the handler contract, for nodes that await async
/// capability imports (RULED wamn-362o.46). The component model permits the
/// `async` canonical option only on an `async func` type -- an async lift of
/// `handler.run` is refused by the validator -- so a node that must await
/// (blob-put over wasmcloud:blobstore's async streams was the first) exports
/// THIS interface, with the same `run` shape typed async, and the router
/// dispatches on whichever handler interface a node exports. Named for what
/// it is, not versioned: nothing outside this tree consumes the node ABI.
/// Sync nodes keep `handler`.
interface async-handler {
  use types.{json, node-context, emission, node-error};

  run: async func(ctx: node-context, input: json) -> result<emission, node-error>;
}

/// Reference world pinning the handler shape over inline JSON. Production
/// components may export several operation-named interfaces with that shape.
/// Effect authority is composed onto a guest rather than enumerated here.
world node {
  export handler;
}

/// Reference world for an async node: the same contract, awaited.
world async-node {
  export async-handler;
}
